.\" Manual page for workforest(5): the configuration file format.
.\" Hand-written roff (man macros); see workforest.1 for the rationale.
.TH WORKFOREST 5 "" workforest "File Formats"
.SH NAME
workforest, wf \- configuration files for workforest
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/workforest/config.yaml
.br
.I ~/.config/workforest/config.yaml
.br
.I .workforest.yaml
.br
.IR .vscode/.workforest.yaml ", " .idea/.workforest.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR workforest (1)
reads its configuration from up to four layers of YAML (or JSON) files.
Every file is optional, every key in it is optional, and later layers
override earlier ones:
.TP
.B system
.I /etc/workforest/config.yaml
\(em organization-wide defaults.
.TP
.B user
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/workforest/config.yaml
(default
.IR ~/.config/workforest/config.yaml )
\(em your terminal and editor setup.
.TP
.B project (shared)
.I .workforest.yaml
in the repository root, resolved against the main worktree \(em
repository policy, committed to the repository.
.TP
.B project (local)
.I .workforest.yaml
inside
.B .vscode/
(checked first) or
.B .idea/
of the main worktree \(em personal overrides, untracked because IDE
folders usually are.
.PP
At each location the first existing basename wins:
.BR config.yaml ", " config.yml ", " config.json
for the global layers and
.BR .workforest.yaml ", " .workforest.yml ", " .workforest.json
for the project layers.
Command-line flags rank above every file.
Nothing in the environment changes the merged result.
.PP
Scalars and lists in a higher layer
.I replace
the lower value.
The
.BR openers ", " wrappers ", and " scripts
mappings
.I merge
per key, and a key set to
.B null
removes the entry inherited from a lower layer.
.PP
.B workforest config
prints the merged result and where each layer came from;
.B workforest init
scaffolds a commented project file
.RB ( \-\-local
for a personal one).
Unknown keys, and
.BR from " or " wrap
names that do not resolve, are reported when the configuration loads.
.SH KEYS
All keys with their built-in defaults:
.PP
.EX
worktrees_dir: "$WF_MAIN/../worktrees/$WF_NAME"
opener: ""
openers: {}
wrappers: {}
symlinks: []
setup_scripts: []
scripts: {}
stop_timeout: 30
.EE
.TP
.B worktrees_dir
Where the forest lives: a path template expanded with
.BR $WF_MAIN ", " $WF_NAME ,
and ordinary environment variables such as
.BR $HOME ,
then normalized.
The per-worktree variables are not available here, since no worktree
exists yet when the directory is resolved.
The default puts a
.B worktrees/
folder next to the main checkout, namespaced per repository:
.I ~/src/api
\(->
.IR ~/src/worktrees/api/NAME .
.TP
.B opener
The default opener for
.BR create " and " open
without
.BR \-o :
an
.B openers
name, or a shell command used verbatim.
Empty means
.BR $VISUAL ,
then
.BR $EDITOR ;
if neither is set, opening fails with a message.
.TP
.B openers
Mapping of name \(-> what
.BI "\-o " NAME
runs (see
.BR "OPENERS AND WRAPPERS" ).
Names also fill the TUI's opener carousel and complete after
.BR \-o .
Environment-specific; belongs in the user layer.
.TP
.B wrappers
Mapping of name \(-> a command that runs an opener somewhere else (a new
terminal window, a tmux window, through
.BR direnv ).
Environment-specific; belongs in the user layer.
.TP
.B symlinks
List of repository-root-relative paths linked from the main worktree
into each new worktree, so expensive or private untracked assets
.RB ( node_modules ", " .env ", " .venv )
are shared instead of rebuilt.
Created links are hidden from
.B git status
in that worktree only.
Missing sources are skipped with a warning; an existing destination that
is not a symlink is never replaced.
.TP
.B setup_scripts
List of shell snippets run in order inside a freshly created worktree.
A failing script warns but does not abort the remaining scripts or the
creation.
Skipped by
.BR "create \-\-no\-hooks" .
.TP
.B scripts
Mapping of name \(-> script for
.BI "workforest run " NAME\fR,
run from the current worktree's root with any extra arguments
shell-quoted and appended.
An entry is a shell command string, or a mapping with exactly one of
.B command
(the shell command),
.B bulk
(a list of entry names to run at once), and
.B pipeline
(a list of entry names to run in order), and optionally
.BR background " and " exclusive
(booleans, default false),
.B cleanup
(a shell command), and
.B stop_timeout
(seconds).
.RS
.TP
.B bulk
The named entries at once, their output relayed line by line and
prefixed with the member's name.
Members cannot read the terminal, but Ctrl-C reaches them all.
A bulk waits for every member and fails if any of them did.
.TP
.B pipeline
The named entries one after another, each with the terminal like a
.B workforest run
of its own; a
.B background
member is started and left running while the pipeline moves on.
The first failing step ends the pipeline with its status.
.PP
A group is run by a supervisor that owns the process group and the
terminal as a command would, so
.BR background ", " exclusive ", " cleanup ", " "workforest stop" ,
and the running record apply to it unchanged;
.B workforest stop
on a group stops every member, waits for all their cleanups, and then
runs the group's own.
A group takes no extra arguments.
Members are named, never written inline, and keep their own settings and
records: a group may name another group (a cycle is a configuration
error),
.B workforest stop
on a member stops it out from under a running group, and a member
already running in the worktree fails its step.
.TP
.B background
Detach by default, as
.B "run \-b"
does: output goes to a log file and
.B workforest
returns once the command is clearly running.
Stop it with
.BR "workforest stop" .
.TP
.B cleanup
Runs after the command ends \(em normal exit, failure, or kill \(em in
the same worktree with the same
.B WF_*
variables.
Its failure is a warning.
.TP
.B exclusive
At most one instance per project: starting the script stops the running
instance in any worktree (this one included) with SIGTERM, then SIGKILL
after
.B stop_timeout
seconds, waits for that instance's cleanup to finish, and only then
starts.
The stopped instance's
.B workforest run
reports who preempted it and exits 143.
Running instances are recorded under
.I .git/workforest/running/
in the main checkout; a record is verified (pid, process group, boot)
before anything is signalled, so a crash, a reboot, or a killed
.B workforest
leaves nothing to tidy by hand.
A command still running after its
.B workforest run
is gone is stopped and cleaned up by the preempting run when
.I /proc
confirms the pid was not recycled; elsewhere it is reported and left
alone.
.TP
.B stop_timeout
This script's grace period between SIGTERM and SIGKILL, overriding the
top-level key; a group's defaults to the longest of its members'.
.RE
.TP
.B stop_timeout
Seconds a stopped script gets to end after SIGTERM before SIGKILL, for
.B workforest stop
and for
.B exclusive
preemption.
A number above 0; default 30.
.SH OPENERS AND WRAPPERS
An
.I opener
is a shell command that runs with the worktree root as working
directory, either
.I in your terminal
(the default: the
shell function changes directory there and runs it) or
.I in the background
(spawned detached;
.B wf
returns at once \(em for GUI applications and commands that hand off to
a daemon or multiplexer).
Optionally it runs
.I through a wrapper\c
, a command that receives it as
.B $WF_COMMAND
and takes it somewhere else.
.PP
An
.B openers
entry is a shell command string, or a mapping with these keys:
.TP
.B command
A shell command \(em always literal, never a name.
.TP
.B from
Another opener's name: reuse its command, and its
.B background
unless set here.
One level only: the target must have a
.B command
of its own.
.TP
.B background
.B true
to spawn the command detached and return immediately.
.TP
.B wrap
A
.B wrappers
name to run the command through.
.PP
An opener has exactly one of
.BR command " and " from .
.BR wrap " and " background
never sit on the same entry: the wrapper decides where the whole thing
runs, through its own
.B background
flag.
.PP
A
.B wrappers
entry is a shell command string, or a mapping with
.BR command " and " background .
It receives the opener command as
.BR $WF_COMMAND ,
still unexpanded, so run it through a shell of its own for its
.B $WF_*
references to resolve:
.BR "$SHELL \-c \(dq$WF_COMMAND\(dq" .
When that shell runs where this environment is not inherited (a tmux
server, an
.BR ssh (1)
host), hand the variable family over as text:
.B \(dqexport $WF_ENV; $WF_COMMAND\(dq
is re-parsed on the far side with quoting intact.
.PP
Background processes shed activation state inherited from the invoking
shell (Python virtualenv, conda, nvm, rvm) so a new window starts clean.
One that dies right away is reported with its stderr.
.PP
Example (user layer):
.PP
.EX
opener: win

wrappers:
  kitty:
    command: kitty \-\-title "$WF_TITLE" \-\-directory "$WF_WORKTREE" $SHELL \-c "$WF_COMMAND"
    background: true
  tmux:
    command: tmux new\-window \-n "$WF_TITLE" \-c "$WF_WORKTREE" "export $WF_ENV; $WF_COMMAND"
    background: true
  direnv: 'direnv exec "$WF_WORKTREE" $SHELL \-c "$WF_COMMAND"'

openers:
  edit: $EDITOR "$WF_TARGET"      # in your terminal
  code:                           # GUI app: detached
    command: code "$WF_WORKTREE"
    background: true
  win: {from: edit, wrap: kitty}  # the editor, in a new kitty window
  pane: {from: edit, wrap: tmux}  # the editor, in a new tmux window
  env: {from: edit, wrap: direnv} # the editor here, through direnv
  git: lazygit
.EE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Openers, wrappers, and scripts are plain shell commands run via
.BR "$SHELL \-c" ,
with one variable family exported \(em the same family the launched
process and every script receive.
Standard shell rules apply; there is no template syntax of workforest's
own:
.B \(dq$WF_X\(dq
is exactly one argument, bare
.B $WF_X
word-splits, and a misspelled variable expands to nothing.
.TP
.B WF_MAIN
Main worktree path, e.g.
.IR /home/user/Projects/api .
.TP
.B WF_NAME
Repository name, e.g.
.IR api .
.TP
.B WF_WORKTREES_DIR
The resolved
.BR worktrees_dir .
.TP
.B WF_WORKTREE
This worktree's path.
.TP
.B WF_BRANCH
Its branch (empty when detached).
.TP
.B WF_TARGET
The
.B \-p
argument, default
.B .
(openers and wrappers only).
.TP
.B WF_TITLE
Window label,
.RI \(dq NAME ": " BRANCH \(dq
(openers and wrappers only).
.TP
.B WF_ENV
All of the above as shell-quoted
.I NAME=value
assignments (openers and wrappers only).
.TP
.B WF_COMMAND
In a wrapper: the opener command, unexpanded.
.PP
.B worktrees_dir
sees only
.BR $WF_MAIN " and " $WF_NAME .
.BR setup_scripts " and " scripts
receive
.BR WF_MAIN ", " WF_NAME ", " WF_WORKTREE ", " WF_WORKTREES_DIR ", and " WF_BRANCH .
.SH EXAMPLE
A project file, committed to the repository:
.PP
.EX
# .workforest.yaml
symlinks: [node_modules, .env]
setup_scripts:
  - npm install \-\-prefer\-offline
scripts:
  test: npm test
  migrate: npm run db:migrate
  frontend:
    command: cd frontend && npm run dev
    exclusive: true
  backend:
    command: docker compose up
    exclusive: true
    background: true
    cleanup: docker compose down
    stop_timeout: 60
  dev:
    bulk: [backend, frontend]
  fresh:
    pipeline: [migrate, dev]
.EE
.SH FILES
Fully commented reference configurations are installed with the package
(for example under
.IR /usr/share/doc/workforest/examples/ ):
.BR config.yaml
for the global layers and
.B workforest.project.yaml
for the project layers.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR workforest (1)
